**Location:** `ActionItemsTile`, lines 624–625

```kotlin
val high   = visible.filter { it.priority.equals("high",   true) }
val medium = visible.filter { it.priority.equals("medium", true) }
val low    = visible.filter { it.priority.equals("low",    true) }
```

Three separate O(n) filter passes over the same list on every recomposition.

**Fix — single partitioning pass + memoize with `remember`:**

```kotlin
val (high, medium, low) = remember(visible) {
    val h = mutableListOf<ActionItem>()
    val m = mutableListOf<ActionItem>()
    val l = mutableListOf<ActionItem>()
    for (item in visible) when (item.priority.lowercase()) {
        "high"   -> h += item
        "medium" -> m += item
        else     -> l += item
    }
    Triple(h, m, l)
}
```

Also, `dismissKey()` is called inside `filter` on every item on every recomposition. Memoize the filtered visible list too:

```kotlin
val dismissed by dismissedActionKeys
val visible = remember(items, dismissed) {
    items?.filter { it.dismissKey() !in dismissed } ?: emptyList()
}
```

---

**Location:** `SourceWebView`, lines 922 & 929; `buildInterceptorJs()` line 966

```kotlin
override fun onPageStarted(...) {
    view?.evaluateJavascript(buildInterceptorJs(), null)   // ← new 370-line string
}
override fun onPageFinished(...) {
    view?.evaluateJavascript(buildHeightFixJs(), null)
    view?.evaluateJavascript(buildInterceptorJs(), null)   // ← again
}
```

`buildInterceptorJs()` constructs the same ~1 KB string every single time. It's called **twice per page load** across 4 WebViews.

**Fix — compute once with `lazy`:**

```kotlin
private val interceptorJs: String by lazy { buildInterceptorJs() }
private val heightFixJs: String by lazy { buildHeightFixJs() }

// In WebViewClient:
override fun onPageStarted(...) { view?.evaluateJavascript(interceptorJs, null) }
override fun onPageFinished(...) {
    view?.evaluateJavascript(heightFixJs, null)
    view?.evaluateJavascript(interceptorJs, null)
}
```

---

**Location:** Line 132 & `LaunchedEffect`, lines 381–383

```kotlin
var globalWebView: WebView? = null   // top-level global

LaunchedEffect(currentTab, webViewMap[currentTab]) {
    globalWebView = webViewMap[currentTab]
}
```

If the Activity is destroyed and recreated, `globalWebView` can hold a reference to a detached WebView from the previous instance, preventing GC. `globalWebView` is also used in `ActionButtons` for the non-Dashboard tab refresh — there's no lifecycle cleanup.

**Fix — clear on Activity destroy and avoid the global entirely:**

```kotlin
// In MainActivity:
override fun onDestroy() {
    super.onDestroy()
    globalWebView = null
    // WebViews in webViewMap are in remember{} and will be GC'd,
    // but explicitly destroy them to release native resources:
    webViewMap.values.forEach { it.destroy() }
}
```

Better still, pass `webViewMap[currentTab]` directly to `ActionButtons` rather than routing through a global.

---

**Location:** `ScrapeBridge.reportData()`, line 153

```kotlin
fun reportData(json: String) {
    Handler(Looper.getMainLooper()).post {   // new Handler every invocation
        // ...
    }
}
```

A new `Handler` is allocated for every data report from every WebView. Under heavy JS activity this creates allocation pressure.

**Fix — single shared handler:**

```kotlin
class ScrapeBridge {
    companion object {
        private val mainHandler = Handler(Looper.getMainLooper())
    }

    @JavascriptInterface
    fun reportData(json: String) {
        mainHandler.post { /* ... */ }
    }
}
```

